```shiploop-state
{
  "evidence_refs": ["Original request S1", "Original request S2", "Exercise context: empty repository and unattended intake"],
  "headline": "Intake established the requested sales summary, test boundary, and defaults for unspecified details.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "A person or script runs the tool from a command line, supplying one or more CSV paths."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "For supplied sales records, standard output shows total revenue, revenue by region, and units by product."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "In scope: a Python CLI that reads sales CSV files and prints the three requested aggregates, plus tests. Out of scope: unrelated delivery features, external integrations, and modifying the source CSV files."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The exercise says the repository is empty, so no existing repository behavior is identified for preservation; discovery should confirm the checkout before implementation."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume `python sales_summary.py FILE [FILE ...]`; UTF-8 CSV files have a header containing `region`, `product`, `units`, and `unit_price`, with extra columns ignored. Revenue is units multiplied by unit_price. Aggregate all files together. Print `Total revenue: AMOUNT`, followed by `Revenue by region:` and sorted `REGION: AMOUNT` lines, then `Units by product:` and sorted `PRODUCT: INTEGER` lines. Amounts use two decimal places. Errors go to standard error; no summary is printed on failure."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Exit 0 on success; exit 2 for missing arguments, unreadable files, malformed CSV, missing or duplicate required headers, or invalid records. Error messages identify the problem and, where applicable, the file and row."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Use exact decimal arithmetic; units are nonnegative integers and unit prices are nonnegative decimal amounts with at most two fractional digits. Trim surrounding whitespace from region and product names. A header-only file contributes zero; processing ends after all files are read or the first error occurs."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Invalid inputs include absent required columns, duplicate required headers, blank region or product names, missing values, negative or non-integer units, negative or non-finite prices, prices with more than two fractional digits, and structurally malformed rows."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The process owns aggregate totals in memory for one invocation; input files remain owned and stored by their existing owner. The tool creates no persistent state."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Summary output is visible to whoever can access the invoking process's standard output; errors use standard error. Concurrent input-file changes have no snapshot or coordination guarantee. A restart rereads the files and recomputes all totals."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume Python 3.10 or newer; discovery should check for an environment constraint before choosing a more specific supported version."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Use the Python standard library for the tool and tests; no third-party dependencies are assumed."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The CLI requires readable CSV paths as arguments and no ports, environment variables, or configuration files."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Keep CSV parsing and validation, aggregation, output formatting, and CLI error handling separable so each can be tested independently."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Use standard-library unittest; run `python -m unittest discover`."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Run the CLI against representative temporary CSV files and check its actual standard output, standard error, and exit status; cover aggregation across files, empty data, and invalid input."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Deliver a locally runnable Python command-line tool in the source checkout; it needs Python and read access to supplied CSV files. No deployment target is named."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "No numeric performance, security, accessibility, or presentation targets are specified. Assume readable plain-text output, exact monetary arithmetic, and no network access requirement."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The largest acceptance risk is that actual sales files use different column names, revenue rules, or output expectations from the recorded defaults."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Early inspection of representative authorized CSV samples or existing schema documentation, followed by a boundary test using that evidence, would expose a mismatch."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1 — Fact: deliver a Python CLI whose user supplies sales CSV files and sees total revenue, revenue per region, and units per product on standard output; assumption: the schema, invocation, calculations, formatting, and errors follow the recorded defaults until evidence says otherwise. Discovery must first establish whether representative authorized files or schema documentation exist; it then resolves column names, revenue semantics, invalid records, and output expectations, which block confident implementation and acceptance checks. Source hints are leads, not verified facts: the team or workspace, relevant channels, internal sites and documentation, private repositories, and sales-data owners; ambiguous terms include sales record, revenue, region, product, and units. Keep any sensitive locator only in an authorized private store, never in committed KNOWLEDGE/. The CLI is non-interactive after invocation: the invoking person or script supplies paths; the runtime supplies process arguments and standard streams; no shared or persistent application state, identity system, or application-level visibility controls are assumed, ruling out cross-session coordination and private per-user views. The isolated source checkout remains at its snapshot until final return, when the delivered source becomes visible there; no deployed target is requested, and any deployed outcome would exist only after release. S2 — Fact: add tests; assumption: standard-library unittest and CLI boundary checks verify the requested aggregates and failure behavior. Discovery should first inspect the actual checkout and runtime for existing constraints; its findings may block the final test command or supported Python version. This intake-only run implements nothing; the repository is described as empty, and no prior run is identified."
}
